    {
      "title": "Control follows the right, not just a name",
      "text": "A single person may be owner, insured and payer, but the roles can be held by different people. Rights are limited by the actual design, irrevocable interests and valid assignments. A cash-value withdrawal right is irrelevant to a term policy without cash value.",
      "example": "A relative who makes a premium payment does not thereby become the owner.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the actual beneficiary-change process",
      "text": "Maryland §16-212 reserves the right to designate or change a beneficiary unless the designation is irrevocable. The policy can require endorsement or insurer acceptance before a change binds the insurer. Required consents and valid assignments must also be considered.",
      "example": "A draft beneficiary form left unsigned in a drawer is not a completed change through the stated process.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Absolute and collateral assignments have different aims",
      "text": "An absolute assignment transfers ownership rights. A collateral assignment grants rights as security for an obligation and can include substantial enforcement rights while reserving others. It does not necessarily change the insured or the named beneficiary. Read the form rather than promising the owner retains unrestricted control.",
      "example": "The carrier’s sample collateral form reserves a beneficiary-change right but grants specified proceeds, surrender and borrowing rights to the assignee, subject to its terms.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Compute the claim from the stated payable amount",
      "text": "The lesson assumes a $100,000 payable benefit, a $20,000 secured amount due, no prior liens and no other deductions. It pays the lender $20,000 and the beneficiary $80,000. A real claim requires checking the secured balance, interest, prior policy loans and assignment priority.",
      "example": "If the secured amount due were $12,000 under those same simplified assumptions, the remaining beneficiary payment would be $88,000.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep application evidence and corrections separate",
      "text": "Under §12-206, an issuance application generally must be attached or otherwise made part of the issued contract to be admissible in a related proceeding, with the stated industrial-life exception. A requested renewal or reinstatement application copy has a separate 30-day delivery rule and stated beneficiary-interest condition. Someone other than the applicant generally cannot alter the written application without written consent; an insurer may make a clearly identified administrative insertion.",
      "example": "An administrative insertion must not falsely appear to be the applicant’s own answer.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.03"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Standard individual provisions have a defined scope",
      "text": "Section 16-201 excludes group life, pure endowments and annuity contracts from this individual-life standard-provision requirement. It also specifies treatment of disability/accidental-death supplementary provisions and inapplicable single-premium or term provisions. Group contracts have separate standard provisions.",
      "example": "Do not automatically apply the individual-life entire-contract statute to every annuity or group question merely because the issuer is a life insurer.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.03"
      ]
    }
